.TH "zprune" "8" "" "" "System Manager\(cqs Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zprune\f[R] \- destroy snapshot prune candidates selected by zelta
prune
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]zprune\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
\f[B]\(enmatch\-endpoint=\f[R]_guard_ \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]zprune\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[B]\(enno\-prune\-guard\f[R]
\f[I]endpoint\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zprune\f[R] destroys snapshots selected by \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
At normal verbosity it prints the grouped \f[CR]zfs destroy\f[R]
commands, runs \f[B]zfs destroy \-nvp\f[R] to estimate reclaim, prints a
summary, asks for confirmation, then destroys the same candidates.
With \f[B]\-qq\f[R] (\f[CR]LOG_LEVEL\f[R] < 1), the dry\-run preview and
summary are skipped for speed.
.PP
Only \f[I]endpoint\f[R] is destroyed.
A match endpoint (\f[I]guard\f[R]) is used only for match validation and
prune\-guard protection.
.PP
Candidate selection, filters, prune guards, and retention options are
identical to \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
See \f[B]zelta\-prune(8)\f[R] for strategy details.
\f[B]zprune\f[R] requires \f[CR]\-\-match\-endpoint\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-no\-prune\-guard\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.SS zprune Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R], \f[CR]\-f\f[R]
Destroy candidates without asking for confirmation.
Combined with \f[B]\-qq\f[R], runs silently aside from errors (suitable
for cron).
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R], \f[CR]\-q\f[R]
Decrease log level.
\f[B]\-q\f[R] hides the destroy command list but still runs the dry\-run
summary.
\f[B]\-qq\f[R] (\f[CR]LOG_LEVEL\f[R] < 1) skips the dry\-run and summary
entirely; without \f[B]\-f\f[R] the confirmation prompt is still shown.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-dryrun\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R]
Preview compact destroy commands and the summary, then exit without
prompting or destroying.
With \f[B]\-qq\f[R], only selects candidates (via \f[B]zelta prune\f[R])
and exits with no output\(emuseful for configuration smoke tests.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R], \f[CR]\-v\f[R]
Increase log level.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-help\f[R], \f[CR]\-h\f[R]
Show command usage.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-version\f[R], \f[CR]\-V\f[R]
Show \f[B]zprune\f[R] version information and exit.
.SS Candidate Selection
All other options are forwarded to \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
See \f[B]zelta\-prune(8)\f[R] for complete behavior, including
\f[CR]\-\-match\-endpoint\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-prune\-num\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-time\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-prune\-grid\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-size\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-depth\f[R].
.SH SAFETY MODEL
\f[B]zprune\f[R] applies these checks before destruction:
.IP \(bu 2
candidates are selected by \f[B]zelta prune\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
candidates are validated before preview;
.IP \(bu 2
at normal verbosity, grouped destroy commands are shown before the
dry\-run estimate;
.IP \(bu 2
candidates are grouped per dataset and previewed with \f[B]zfs destroy
\-nvp\f[R] (skipped when \f[CR]LOG_LEVEL\f[R] < 1);
.IP \(bu 2
destruction uses the same grouped candidate form as the preview;
.IP \(bu 2
the prompt summarizes snapshot count and estimated reclaimed space
(unless \f[B]\-qq\f[R]);
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-dryrun\f[R] shows compact destroy commands and summary, then
exits before prompting;
.IP \(bu 2
the operator must answer \f[CR]y\f[R] or \f[CR]yes\f[R] unless
\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] is used;
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]zfs destroy \-R\f[R] is never used;
.IP \(bu 2
only \f[I]endpoint\f[R] is destroyed; the match endpoint is never
destroyed.
.PP
If \f[I]endpoint\f[R] is remote, destruction runs on that host through
\f[B]ZELTA_REMOTE_COMMAND\f[R] (default \f[B]ssh\f[R]).
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview default candidates and confirm before destroying:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
Destroy without the confirmation prompt:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-force \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
Preview destroy commands and summary without prompting:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-dryrun \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
Print only the dry\-run summary:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-qn \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
Silent forced prune (no dry\-run, no prompt\(emcron\-friendly):
.IP
.EX
zprune \-qqf \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
Apply a retention strategy and destroy (same options as \f[B]zelta
prune\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-prune\-grid=\(aq30x1day, 52x1week, 1year\(aq \(rs
    \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
Without a match endpoint, pass \f[CR]\-\-no\-prune\-guard\f[R]
explicitly:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-no\-prune\-guard \-\-prune\-size=10G tank/data
.EE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
\f[B]zprune\f[R] bootstraps through \f[B]zelta\f[R], so
\f[B]ZELTA_ENV\f[R] settings such as \f[B]PRUNE_GUARD\f[R] are loaded
before candidates are selected.
See \f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R] and \f[B]zelta\-prune(8)\f[R].
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and non\-zero on error, aborted confirmation,
failed candidate preview, or failed destruction.
.SH NOTES
For non\-destructive candidate reporting, use \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
To preview the exact destroy operations \f[B]zprune\f[R] would run, use
\f[CR]zprune \-\-dryrun\f[R].
